<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tengrism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tengrism is the modern name for the traditional religion of the Turkic and Mongol peoples of the Eurasian steppe, centred on Tengri, the god of the sky, together with the earth, spirits of nature and ancestors, and shamans who mediated with them. Rulers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> claimed their authority from Tengri. Many steppe peoples later adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, but its practices persisted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tengrism.jpg" alt="Tengrism" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SiGarb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peak_of_Khan_Tengri_at_sunset.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Khazar society was marked by religious pluralism: Judaism, Christianity, Islam and traditional <b>Tengrist</b> beliefs were all practised, which eased commercial dealings with a varied range of partners.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion included <b>Tengrism</b> and Manichaean and Buddhist influences, and they burned their dead.</blockquote><a href="/state/kimek-khanate">Kimek Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
